// The first example in LAYR's documentation (docs/content/start/introduction.md), unchanged.
const example = `Page(
  .name(Profile)
  .route('/')

  var int likes = 0

  Scaffold(
    .config(color: canvas)
    .body(
      Column(
        .config(
          w: fill
          padding: all(32)
          xAlign: mid
        )
        Row(
          .config(
            color: panel
            cornerRadius: 16
            gap: 16
            padding: all(20)
            yAlign: mid
            .border(
              color: line
              width: 1
            )
          )
          Container(
            .config(
              size: 56
              color: accent
              cornerRadius: 999
            )
          )
          Column(
            .config(gap: 2)
            Text(
              .config(
                size: 18
                weight: semibold
              )
              .obj('Ada Lovelace')
            )
            Text(
              .config(color: muted)
              .obj('Wrote the first published program, in 1843')
            )
            Text(
              .config(
                size: 13
                color: faint
              )
              .obj('$likes people like this')
            )
          )
          Button(
            .preset(default)
            .config(label: 'Like')
            .fnc { likes++ }
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)`;

const start = `npm i -g @dynshift/layr
layr create my-app
cd my-app
npm install
layr dev`;
---

<p>
  LAYR is a language for the layout of web pages. Its name is an acronym,
  <strong>Layout Authoritative Yet Responsive</strong>, and that phrase is a fair
  summary of the idea: the layout you write is the layout you get, and it still
  adapts to every screen. You describe what the page <em>is</em> (objects inside
  objects, measured in the numbers from your design) and a compiler turns that
  into static CSS and React components, checking it before anything runs.
</p>
<p>
  LAYR 3.0 was released on 25 September 2026 and the current version is 3.3.0.
  It is open source under the MIT licence and published on npm as
  <code>@dynshift/layr</code>. This article covers what problem it addresses, what
  a LAYR file looks like, the ideas it is built on, and who it is for.
</p>

<h2>The problem</h2>
<p>
  Building a page from a design is mostly an act of translation. A designer draws
  a frame 390 pixels wide with a 24-pixel gap and a 16-pixel corner radius. A
  developer then turns those numbers into rems, viewport units,
  <code>clamp()</code> expressions and media queries, picks a flexbox or grid
  arrangement, and decides what should happen at each breakpoint. Somewhere in
  that translation the original intent is lost. Reading the stylesheet back, it
  is hard to see the page; reading the page, it is hard to say why it behaves the
  way it does at 700 pixels wide.
</p>
<p>
  The usual remedies each solve part of it. Utility-class frameworks are quick to
  write and hard to read back. Component libraries bring a layout system along
  with buttons, forms and a visual theme that may not be wanted. Neither makes the
  structure of the page and the structure of the code the same thing.
</p>

<h2>What it looks like</h2>
<p>
  Here is a complete LAYR page, the first example in the documentation. It draws
  a profile row: a coloured circle, three lines of text and a button that counts
  likes.
</p>

<CodeBlock code={example} lang="layr" />

<p>
  It reads from top to bottom in the shape of the result. A <code>Page</code>
  has a name and an address. <code>var int likes = 0</code> is state that the page
  remembers; anything that reads it updates when it changes. A
  <code>Scaffold</code> is the screen, and its <code>.body</code> is a slot, the
  place where content goes. <code>Column</code> and <code>Row</code> stack their
  objects down and across. <code>.config(...)</code> sets an object's features,
  and colour names such as <code>panel</code> and <code>accent</code> come from the
  theme. The button's <code>.fnc</code> runs when it is pressed: round brackets
  hold LAYR, and curly braces hold TypeScript.
</p>
<p>
  The number <code>56</code> deserves a closer look. It is not 56 CSS pixels. It
  is 56 pixels <em>of the design frame</em>, and LAYR scales it to fit whatever
  screen the page is on. And on a narrow phone, when the text would be squeezed,
  the row stacks into a column by itself. Nothing in the code asks for that.
</p>

