<h2>The ideas it is built on</h2>

<h3>The code's shape is the page's shape</h3>
<p>
  Objects nest the way they appear on screen. Logic is kept in clearly marked
  places (<code>.config</code>, <code>.fnc</code> and TypeScript blocks) so that
  the tree of objects stays readable as a picture of the page.
</p>

<h3>Design units, not CSS units</h3>
<p>
  LAYR calls its unit model <strong>Design Scale</strong>. A project declares
  design frames, by default a 390-pixel phone, an 834-pixel tablet, a 1440-pixel
  desktop and a 2560-pixel ultrawide, and numbers in LAYR files are pixels of
  whichever frame is active. A single value scales smoothly with the screen
  within its frame; a value given for several frames is interpolated between
  them; and structural choices, such as which way a row runs or whether something
  is shown at all, switch at the frame boundaries. There are no media queries to
  write.
</p>

<h3>Deterministic layout</h3>
<p>
  Every width and height is one of three kinds: a fixed length, <code>hug</code>
  (fit the content, the default) or <code>fill</code> (share the space the parent
  has left). When space runs out, a row adapts in a fixed, documented order:
  flexible content shrinks and text wraps first; then a row of small items wraps
  onto new lines, while a row containing a filling pane stacks into a column; and
  only when nothing can adapt does content clip, with a compiler warning. The
  <code>layr analyze</code> command can explain any adaptation it made.
</p>

<h3>Reachable from anywhere, protected by default</h3>
<p>
  Any part of a LAYR app can read, change or copy any object's features, from
  any page or widget, through four operations together called
  <strong>EIED</strong>: Export, Inject, Extract and Duplicate. Objects are
  protected by default, and using one of these operations is the explicit way in.
  Changes are ordered, reversible layers that the compiler knows about, and
  anything marked <code>!mut</code> cannot be changed from outside at all.
  <a href="/articles/how-layr-works/">How LAYR works</a> goes into this in detail.
</p>

<h3>One canonical form</h3>
<p>
  LAYR accepts shorthand and aliases while you type, but
  <code>layr format</code> writes every file the same way, so code reads the same
  in every project, and AI agents that write LAYR have one correct form to
  follow.
</p>

<h2>Written by people and by agents</h2>
<p>
  LAYR is designed to be written by people and by AI coding agents, and several
  of its choices suit an agent better than hand-written HTML, CSS and React do.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>One answer instead of many.</strong> The same layout can be written
    dozens of ways in CSS (flexbox or grid, margins or gaps, rems or pixels, a
    media query here and a <code>clamp()</code> there), and an agent has to guess
    which one a project uses. LAYR has one canonical form, and
    <code>layr format</code> rewrites anything else into it.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The result follows from the code.</strong> Every feature resolves in
    one documented order, and layout adapts to a smaller screen by fixed rules
    that <code>layr analyze</code> can explain. What a page does on a phone is
    something the code determines, not something found out by looking.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Mistakes come back as instructions.</strong> Problems are diagnostics
    with a code, <code>layr analyze --json</code> lists them in a form a program
    can read, and <code>layr explain</code> describes each one. They are precise
    enough for an agent to correct its own work, the way a person would.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The reference ships with the language.</strong>
    <code>layr skills install</code> adds the LAYR Skill to a project: the rules
    of the language and a reference for every widget, key and diagnostic,
    generated from the compiler's own schema and matched to the installed
    version. It also registers an MCP server whose tools look up widgets, check a
    snippet and analyse the project, and the documentation is published as
    <code>llms.txt</code> for agents that read the web.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  None of this is a separate mode. It is the same compiler, formatter and
  diagnostics a person uses, which is the point: an agent and a person working
  on the same project are checked by the same rules.
</p>

<h2>What you get</h2>
<div class="table-scroll">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The language</td><td><code>.layr</code> files: pages, widgets, functions, state and layout</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The compiler</td><td>Checks names, types, layout and every EIED operation before anything runs, then emits CSS and React</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The runtime</td><td>Signals, the injection cascade, measurement, animation and routing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The CLI</td><td><code>layr create</code>, <code>dev</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>format</code>, <code>analyze</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>upgrade</code> and more</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tooling</td><td>A VS Code extension, a language server, an MCP server and a Skill that teaches AI agents the language</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2>How it fits with React</h2>
<p>
  LAYR compiles to React components, so React is the engine underneath, but not
  the way LAYR is written. The two work in both directions: a LAYR app can use any
  React package, even as JSX written directly in the tree, and an existing React
  app can import <code>.layr</code> files. That makes it possible to adopt LAYR
  one page or one component at a time.
</p>

<h2>Who it is for</h2>
<p>
  LAYR suits people who build websites from designs and want the numbers in the
  design to be the numbers in the code: designers who write their own front
  ends, developers who work closely with design files, and small teams who would
  rather read a layout than reverse-engineer one. Developers coming from Flutter
  will find the object tree, the slots and the hug-and-fill sizing familiar.
</p>
<p>
  It is less suited to projects that are mostly application logic with little
  layout, or teams heavily invested in a particular CSS methodology who are happy
  with it. LAYR is static-first: <code>layr build</code> prerenders pages to
  static files, which is how its own website is built and deployed.
</p>

<h2>Where it came from</h2>
<p>
  The name has a history. LAYR 1.x, released in December 2025, was a small
  library of React components that brought Flutter's layout widgets, such as
  <code>Container</code>, <code>Row</code> and <code>Column</code>, to React.
  LAYR 3 keeps the name and much of that vocabulary, but it is a different kind
  of thing: a language with its own compiler, runtime and tooling. It replaced
  the component library as the default <code>@dynshift/layr</code> on npm, and
  there was never a public LAYR 2. LAYR 1.x remains installable as
  <code>@dynshift/layr@v1</code> and lives on its own branch of the repository,
  with a migration guide in the documentation for projects moving across.
</p>

